WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.990 align:start position:0%
 
we begin with fast-moving developments  about the coronavirus including the  first reported death in canada an  elderly man in british columbia a  resident in a care home has died of  covid 19. it was a deadly turning point  for public health in the  the finger pointing began as soon as the  body started being carried out of the  residence 31 deaths in a couple of weeks  that's something very urgent the  coronavirus spread like wildfire  residents were dying in their beds more  than sixteen thousand long-term care  residents in canada lost their lives to  covet nineteen  fast forward to today and one thing has  changed thanks to vaccines the death  rate from covet in care homes has  plummeted what remains a crisis though  is staffing there was a shortage before  and the pandemic has made it worse  to get a clearer picture i met with  karen biggs she's a registered nurse and  ceo of meadow place in abbotsford bc one  of the largest seniors care campuses in  the province  for her the job isn't just professional  it's personal  hey sweetie you're going to be on tv  karen biggs has worked in geriatric care  for years and has done it all or she  thought she had then the pandemic hit i  can honestly say that in my 46 years of  nursing it's been the hardest time of my  career so this is that oh it is she runs  meno place a sprawling campus of six  buildings that offers multiple levels of  care from independent living to 24 7  nursing home care to 700 people  including peter he is karen's husband  peter moved in here in july and i had  had him at home and i found the care was  too much for me at the time peter  collapsed two and a half months after  their wedding in early 2020.  he had a brain tumor  after surgery to remove it he got  covered in the hospital  karen was living a nightmare trying to  keep him and all the residents in the  place she worked alive it would break my  heart when i saw people coming to our  windows to wave at their parents  everyone went into strict isolation some  families she said complained meno place  which long prided itself on being warm  and welcoming became like a prison we  had to hold families away  which was very hurtful for for us and  for the families and and we we wore the  wrath of many families because they  didn't understand you know why are you  not letting us come to see our loved  ones and what's it been like for staff  it's been really hard for staff we had  the single site order we lost 39 staff  to that the single site order prohibited  staff from working in multiple care  homes something that was common before  covid for nine months they held on and  kept covet out of meadow place  then like a ghost it appeared you'll  know in those days we didn't know that  it was airborne  and so it started with one resident who  came back from the acute care hospital  covered positive and we didn't know  because she was only there five hours  and every two days we got another  positive and another positive and  another positive  and at the end of that one  we had lost 16 dear seniors it was  profoundly upsetting to us  because you're so attached to the  residents you know they become your  family and in those days we had no  visitors  so our staff were the only people that  were seeing those seniors even when they  were dying  even when they were dying in those days  25 staff also got covered the shortage  was so acute patient care suffered  bathing was less frequent residents were  left in bed all day karen a problem  solver had an idea why not hire and pay  family members of residence i was  absolutely desperate because at that  time we had an outbreak and we had to  put in extra enhanced cleaning and we  just we just didn't have anybody she  ended up hiring nine family members you  went through  some terrible times the staffing  shortage still exists it got worse  you'll remember and that's what the  protests are about right now you  remember mandatory vaccinations so we  then again  lost nine regulars and 29 casuals  because we had people who refused to get  the vaccine  what did some of the staff  who refused to be vaccinated tell you  about their reasons i talked to one lpn  at least 10 times myself as the ceo  trying to get her to stay because she  was a really good caregiver of my  husband this is a licensed practical  nurse right who and she looked after  your husband yeah and she was so kind  and i did not want to see her leave and  she was a single mom  and so i tried every argument in the  book and she said no i read something  that a doctor in the united states put  on facebook  that it's going to alter my dna  and i said well you know there's many  learned people that don't believe that  way and i would just urge you to read  what they've written  but i couldn't convince her i couldn't  convince her that that one story wasn't  the true story  i actually support the position for  mandatory vaccination because the risk  is too great for our seniors we had an  outbreak last september on a unit where  everybody was double vaccinated the good  thing is we didn't have people die this  time what did losing those staff do to  care for residents  well it definitely added pressure on all  the all the staff that were left people  work overtime even when they don't want  to because they can't leave their  colleagues to be working short and they  can't leave the care not to be done we  have a full-time recruiter that we've  had for a year and a half now we've  recruited 222 people in the last year  but we're still desperately short the  hardest to recruit she says are not  personal support workers it's nurses she  doesn't think pay is the problem at  least not in bc the salaries are  excellent you know if you look as i  carry they're making twenty five dollars  an hour you know our nurses are making  uh probably about 55 an hour and the  benefits are amazing so karen what's the  solution here to recruiting one of the  mandates that we've been pushing in the  federal government is to try to make it  easier for internationally trained  nurses to come to canada  we have got wonderful internationally  trained nurses working as carried  because they can't get the paperwork  through to work as nurses here in canada  how acute is this staffing shortage more  broadly beyond your facility well it's  not only our province it's national you  know when i do accreditation surveying  in ontario and quebec and alberta's just  in the same situation the pandemic she  says may have scared people away from  working in care homes and has burned out  nurses everywhere what would you say to  them to  make long-term care and appealing  career the  rewards of working with seniors are are  absolutely immense if you love people if  you love stories if you love  hearing about the wonderful things that  they've done with their lives we weep  when they're sick we weep when they die  you know we become their family and  that's a precious precious gift you know  finish having your lunch one blessing of  having her husband at the care home  karen says is that she has him close by  every day the challenges aren't over but  he's starting to eat he's starting to  talk and hopefully one day he'll get him  walking again okay sweetie pie i have to  go to a meeting  give me a hug  you know she's constantly working on  solutions knowing the need for carers  will only grow as we all get  older love you

